# Introduction

* Decision Pipeline
- The Decision Pipeline Framework
  - From raw data to prediction to causal effect estimation to
    utility-maximizing policy
  - Feedback loops and learning from outcomes
  - Why each stage matters and what breaks when stages are skipped

* Audience and Approach
- Explain the audience and the philosophy of the book

- Target Audience
  - Senior ML engineers and data scientists with a statistics and probabilistic ML
    background who build production decision systems
  - Working knowledge of causal basics (DAGs, SCMs, do-calculus) assumed
  - The needed theory and background is assumed already understood and summarized
    surveyed to level set

- Approach of the book
  - Focus on:
    - The minimal mathematics to understand the problem and the solutions
    - Intuition
    - Toy examples
    - How to make the theory operational
      - Referring to packages in the Python ecosystem
    - Jupyter notebooks to back up the intuition with toy and complex examples

  - Provide resources to go one level deep
    - More detailed technical slides on the topics
    - References to books and papers

* A book in the age of AI

- What matters is "taste", reviewing for clarity, creating a path for learning based
  on what research and industry requires, tutorials that bridge the theory with the
  intuition
- Trying to explain concepts to make it easy for a human to understand
- Took me 12 months to put this together

- Book is a subset of content
  - Each content is available in the form of slides (without all the chit-chat)
  - Each slide set is around 30 slides which corresponds to a chapter of
    a technical book (~1000 words)
  - Tutorials are available for each slide set
  - Book makes reference to examples in the slides

// TODO: See website/docs/blog/posts/draft.Writing_Books_For_Humans_and_AI.md

* Book Roadmap

- How This Book Is Organized
  - Five-part structure: motivation, theory, data, algorithms, deployment/governance
  - Intended audience and prerequisites
  - How chapters build on each other and fit into the decision pipeline

- **Key idea**: this book walks a single pipeline end-to-end, from data to action
  $$data \to model \to policy \to action \to feedback$$
  - The model is both causal and probabilistic
- The book is organized in five parts:
  - **Part I**: why businesses need decisions, not predictions
  - **Part II**: probabilistic and causal foundations, including Gaussian
    processes, variational inference, normalizing flows, and causal discovery
  - **Part III**: single-step decisions, including decision theory, policy
    learning, and partial identification
  - **Part IV**: multi-step and dynamic decisions, including causal world
    models, causal reinforcement learning, and feedback loops
  - **Part V**: deploying, monitoring, and governing decision systems in
    production
- This lesson opens Part I by cataloging what traditional machine learning
  leaves out

- The sequence of the parts in the books are:
  - Motivation
    - 01, Introduction
    - 02, Why Decisions, Not Predictions
    - 03, the Cost of Ignoring Causality, Uncertainty and Dynamics
  - Advanced Modeling Theory & Tools
    - 04, Knowledge Representation
    - 05, Probabilistic ML
    - 06, Causal ML
  - Data
    - 07, Building Causal Knowledge
    - 08, Causal data pipelines
  - Decision-Making Theory & Tools
    - 09, Decision Theory Foundations
    - 10, Taxonomy of Decision-Making Problems and Algorithms
    - 11, Simple Decisions
    - 12, Complex Decisions
    - 13, Agentic Causal Reasoning
  - Implementation, Deployment, & Governance
    - 14, Building Stakeholder Alignment
    - 15, Deployment, Monitoring, and Adaptation
    - 16, Trust, Explainability, Fairness, and Governance
